    if ( LINUX && ARM )
        libname += "arm" + ABI;
    if ( !x64 )
        libname += "32";

    this.file = System.mapLibraryName(libname);
}
```

So if `x64` is true, the libname stays as is (e.g. `jemalloc` → `jemalloc.dll`), otherwise it appends "32" (e.g. `jemalloc32` → `jemalloc32.dll`).

The user is on a 64-bit Windows OS and gets `jemalloc32.dll` error. That means the `x64` check failed. The `x64` property is `osArch.contains("64")`. On 64-bit Windows with a 64-bit JVM, `os.arch` should be `amd64` which contains "64". 

